--TEST--
`Core\Log::write` refuses a `fields` value with no JSON encoding and writes nothing at all for that record -- ADR 0020 § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `fields` is `array<string, mixed>`, so a value JSON cannot spell reaches the
// encoder -- and it is the same encoder `Core\Json::encode` uses, so it
// refuses the same values with the same sentence. ADR 0009 § 1's `bytes` is
// one of them: it is not a string that happens to be binary, it is its own
// type, and a text format has nothing to write it as.
array<mixed> $binary = ["payload" => "text" as bytes];
try {
    Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, "unwritable", $binary);
    echo "written", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// And nothing half-written was left behind. A record is serialised whole and
// handed to the output stream in one call, so the line after a refusal is the
// next record rather than the tail of the one that failed.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, "after");
--EXPECT--
Core\Log::write(): a `bytes` value has no JSON encoding
{"level":"info","msg":"after"}
